WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.960
JENNY BROWN: Today on
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.480
we're going to use
our imaginations.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.075
So picture a security camera.

00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:08.985
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.560
Picture a security camera
capturing and storing footage

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.660
in case something goes wrong.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.270
Every half second, the
camera takes and uploads

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.490
a photo to local
storage, conveniently

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.980
named with the time
stamp of the photo.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.020
Batches of those
photos are regularly

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.850
uploaded to a
Google Cloud bucket

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.870
for display and analysis.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.990
But there's trouble.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.510
And no, not security trouble.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.450
There's a backlog forming
from these uploads.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.890
It's taking so long to upload
the local images on the camera

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.440
that they aren't getting
cleared out in time.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.020
Delays like this max
out the local memory

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.120
and make the camera unreliable.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.850
And that is some big trouble.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.355
You've seen the
show before, so you

00:00:52.355 --> 00:00:53.730
know there are
several things you

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.820
can do to prevent slowdowns,
like using parallel uploads

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.150
for small files.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.730
But for this,
we're going to have

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.650
to dive in and learn
a little bit more

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.260
about how cloud storage
works under the hood

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.060
so that we can see what
else might be going on.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.860
Let's start with what happens
when GCS uploads files.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.140
When uploading a file
to cloud storage,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.930
our friend the load balancer
is responsible for handling

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.570
the upload by deciding how
to distribute connections

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.100
between shards.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.710
The secret sauce here is
that the load balancer

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.100
uses file names to
guide that distribution.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.980
This means that the more
diverse the file names,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.650
the better the system
can balance the load.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.680
And conversely, the more
similar, the harder it

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.270
is to reach peak performance.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.720
This means that if you have
files that are sequentially

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.690
named, like, say,
with a time stamp,

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.810
you're going to run into
this issue in a big way,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.940
and that is exactly our issue.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.060
The camera is using the
time stamp as the file name,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.290
running right into this
performance problem.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.720
Fortunately, the fix
is pretty simple.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.050
We need to add some
noise to our file names.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.070
That's right, noise,
a pseudo random hash

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.890
of the file name prepended
to the file name, which

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.500
will allow GCS to
better partition

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.139
the fixed range into
shards, improving our upload

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:13.030
performance without
clobbering our data.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.300
This will almost certainly
get rid of that headache,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.640
and there's more to come
about optimizing performance.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.050
But we're going
to do you a favor

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.090
and save that for another time.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.000
If you want to know more about
file naming and its impact,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.860
check out the links
in the description.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.530
And don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.560
and tell us what you want to
learn about cloud storage.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.440
Thanks for joining us for this
quick byte of cloud storage.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.280
See you next time.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

